y he kind of created  all of this he told me that thread who  is academic and professional career he  saw almost no research when it came to  video game addiction mainly because the  people that he was working with and  reported  - didn't know jack [ __ ] about video  games you know there are some smart  knowledgeable people who are experts in  the field of Psychiatry there and so I  started asking them about video game  addiction and this was about five years  ago now and was surprised that despite  how knowledgeable and brilliant they are  they just didn't understand it and if  you look at people who are leaders in  the field of especially medicine and  psychiatry they're all older like if you  think about Chiefs of departments of  Psychiatry there they're in their 50s  60s or 70s and none of these people have  played video games and so I sort of  realized like no one really knows what's  going on here the other reason well that  actually goes back to his own college  experience see well these days he might  be a successful psychiatrist with a  private practice and now this weird  twitch career at one point he almost  flunked out of college because video  games dominated his life I struggled  with video game addiction or playing too  many video games started in high school  really became a problem in college when  I was failing just about all of my  classes was on academic probation had  less than a 2.0 GPA and basically woke  up every day started to play video games  and played video games all day went to  sleep early early in the morning like 3  a.m. 4 a.m. and just really wasn't going  anywhere in life so on one hand he saw  absolutely nobody talking about these  issues while he also dealt with these  problems in his own life and so he  decided to do something about it  so he's got a website with a blog the  YouTube channel with a lecture series  that's pretty goddamn funny and then the  twitch channel where he talks to  everybody from the little guy to some  big names like Devin Nash reckful and  destiny this whole thing starts with its  discord server there's channels  dedicated to the topics he discusses  regularly on stream and there's even  this like assignments tab where right  now for example you can write 500 words  about what you think is wrong with the  world  I don't think 500 words is enough and  then the twitch stream starts and it's a  long conversation about honestly a lot  of the topics that we hear about when it  comes to streaming and twitch you so the  first thing is let's just think a little  bit about kind of like a  addictive personalities so a lot of  people that I work with say that they  have an addictive personality and if you  think about it you know they'll say like  okay I used to be addicted to like video  games and now I'm addicted to like sex  or I'm that's not gonna happen  things like burnout and social anxiety  and getting over toxic relationships to  even eSports pros dealing with the  pressure that comes with having that  kind of career I prepare with every  stream and I kind of say like okay  here's an opportunity for me to help one  person I have one hour of my life and  they have one hour of their life and I  don't know what this person needs  because I don't really prepare like I  don't usually talk to people ahead of  time or anything like that it's just I  just show up and they show up and then  the main question that I asked is what  does this person need and oftentimes  that is not a canned answer because  people are different it's kind of like  one big therapy session but somehow it's  entertaining and really relatable that  being said he emphasized the fact that  all of this doesn't replace actual  therapy and well the twitch channel has  been steadily growing  it's a December first interview with  reckful that actually caused its  popularity to go through the [ __ ]  roof reckful is a former world of  warcraft pro and obviously a member of  trainwreck TV and he's also been very  open about his mental health issues on  social media and his words spread about  this psychiatrist streaming on Twitch  his fans thought it would be good for  the two of them to connect at almost two  hours long the interview was extensive  and includes like really relatable  moments like this so tell me what your  experience of depression is okay it's  very hard very hard to explain the  easiest way to explain is I've had years  of my life like this where I wake up  every day and I don't see a purpose for  doing anything and this moment  specifically went viral on Reddit what  do you like about what you're doing I  like I like that I'm trying to give  people something I wanted as a kid what  is what is the who's the focus on who  are you thinking about  [Music]  just people have an experience yeah so  I'll tell you reckful listen carefully  now your depression you're trying to  cure the depression that is growing  within kids outs out there today you're  trying to protect them from what  happened to you dr. K's twitch channel  blew up raffle seems to be doing better  and there was like a million articles  written about this dude I'm I'm happy I  can say with confidence I am actually  day-to-day happy even when I'm doing  nothing and alone I'm happy I've never  felt this way in my life okay I've never  felt day-to-day happy when I'm doing  nothing and alone and I was able to chat  with dr. K about the impact of that big  episode I did the same thing that I do  every time on stream and sometimes I  have to like remind myself that this is  what I'm doing  it's just show up and try to help the  person right I've got one hour with this  guy you know whether he's famous or not  famous or a streamer or just a regular  person like it makes no difference to me  like I have one hour with this person  let's see what we can do and how can I  help how can I be of service  that's my time' that's my duty or  responsibility we hear about burnout and  mental health issues associated with  gaming all the time it's something miss  kip recently talked about t foods talked  about it it's something that eSports  pros talk about all the time as well I  wasn't really but I mean like when I  went to the war chat I I made it seem a  lot easier dude don't cry my sweet tank  it was hard at getting out of the war  and for July and August in September if  I put on such an act on stream that I  was happy when I was miserable every  like I would get off stream and I was  either it was so weird I would get off  stream and I wouldn't talk now obviously  streaming alone in your room for like 12  hours a day can be a really lonely job  and demands a lot of time and now we  have the psychiatrist who literally went  to Harvard streaming on Twitch and  talking about these issues that in part  were caused by the job itself it's like  this weird self-fulfilling prophecy  snake eating its tail kind of thing  and yeah the healthy gamer twitch  channel is growing faster than ever and  dr. K has received insane amounts of  requests to chat on stream and so I  asked him about this difficult  contradiction and so when it comes to  like isn't it ironic that twitch is  causing all these problems and you're  using twitch to help people well yeah  and also like yeah right because we want  to use twitch like technology isn't evil  it's just what you do with it  and I don't think that people have  realized the psychological impact that  being a YouTube influencer or a twitch  streamer has and now that people are  getting wise you have people like me who  are trying to help them and my goal is  that you know we'll be able to help  twitch streamers like be healthy and  happy and also like the gamers watching  at home be healthy and happy think about  it like this right dr. K is objectively  helping people but now there's an  audience expecting something and so how  do you balance those two things I  started noticing things within myself  that I imagine other streamers struggle  with so like when we started to get more  viewers I caught myself looking at my  viewer account and I caught myself  wanting to grow that viewer count oh  that's interesting my mind is  gravitating towards wanting me to have a  bigger viewer count because then I feel  like I'm doing a better job so I like  caught myself and I was like oh actually  that's not healthy at all because there  is this performative acts aspect there  is this sort of sense that I don't want  to disappoint people on stream right and  I think that like that's actually one of  the toughest things is that people now  have an expectation that they're gonna  come on stream and within an hour an  hour and a half I'm gonna do something  magical to transform their lives yeah  I do not care if you don't like Bugs  Bunny and Tweety like that whole well I  don't know what to say that you know  like [ __ ] YouTube it it's all over  YouTube you know

